  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several kinds of behavioral and cognitive experiments used in animals, especially rodents and monkeys, and links them to tasks shown on a computer. Across the panels, animals are placed in controlled boxes or arenas and exposed to stimuli while researchers measure their responses. One set of images shows a mouse before and after an aversive event, suggesting fear or pain-related conditioning with sound or shock cues. Another shows a monkey viewing a snake, emphasizing visual presentation of biologically important stimuli and the animal’s reaction.\n\nA separate panel depicts a human-like observer watching the animal image on a monitor, implying video-based stimulus presentation or observation of social or emotional cues. The maze-like arena with a mouse indicates navigation or movement tracking before and after an aversive stimulus. The two monkeys sharing space near a bowl of food illustrate social decision-making or reward-related interactions between individuals. Finally, the computer screen labeled with another’s choice suggests a task in which one subject responds based on the decision of a partner, pointing to social learning, observational choice, or other-regarding behavior.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys a toolbox of paradigms for studying emotion, perception, learning, and social cognition across species.",
